WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:07.306
In the U.S., about a third
of adults aren't getting
enough sleep.

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:10.143
That's defined as less
than seven hours per night.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:13.212
For teenagers,
that number is over 70%.

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:14.647 line:0%
But if you turn on the TV

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.050 line:0%
and look at the way
that we talk about sleep,

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.285 line:0%
you can kind of see why.

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:24.123 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Rich people don't sleep!
- My wife was so good,
  I didn't want to sleep!

00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:26.626
I slept for two, three hours
a night and I had a blast.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:30.329
I feel like I function fine
on four to five hours of sleep.

00:00:30.362 --> 00:00:32.498
When things get really intense,
I don't have time to go home

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:34.167
and shower and change,
so I just sleep here.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.470
We live with this bizarre
culture around sleep,

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.473
and I think I get enough of it
in spite of that.

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:41.841
But I want to
better understand

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:44.377
what's happening
to my body when I don't.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.679
So what happens to us
when we lose sleep?

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:49.416
And why do so many of us
struggle to get enough of it?

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.086 line:0%
<i> ( alarm beeping )</i>

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:56.222 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:04.530
So, for the past few weeks,
I've been recording

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:07.533
my sleep data
with a fitness tracker.

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.268
But to take that
to the next level,

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:10.803
I just picked up
an at home sleep test

00:01:10.836 --> 00:01:12.238
that'll really
help me determine

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:15.241
just how healthy
my sleep actually is.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.446
So this kit is going
to monitor five things--

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.516
the air coming out of my nose,
my brain activity,

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.886
my blood oxygen level,
my chest movements,
and my heart rate.

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:31.757
I really feel like I'm going
into the matrix right now.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:35.128
This is gonna be really
uncomfortable to sleep tonight,

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:38.231
which I think is the part
I'm most nervous about.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.733
Only when I've done
all five things

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:43.236
will all of those lights
light up.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.371
I'm struggling
with this whole thing.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.510
There we go. It's all green.
You see that?

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:53.880
It is 12:28.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.581
I think I'm gonna try
to go to sleep

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:57.784
and see how this goes.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:01.521
Good morning.
Oh, my God.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:06.626
Um, where do I start?
That was tough.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.396
Very uncomfortable to sleep
with all of this stuff on.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:13.599
Part of me is worried
because it wasn't a very normal

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.601
representative night
of sleep.

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:18.471
So, the next step for me
is I take this thing
to a sleep doctor

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.273
in New York,
Dr. Mayank Shukla,

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.376
and he tells me exactly
how healthy my sleep is.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.653 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Hello. How's it going?
- Hi. Good morning.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you for coming in today.
I guess you are here
for your sleep test result.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.326 align:start size:97% position:3%
How do you feel about
the night that you were putting

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:42.862 align:start size:75% position:25%
the sleep test device on
compared to all of the,

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:45.298 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
you know, nights that
you've been sleeping?

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.933 align:start position:0% line:0%
Yeah, it went okay.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:49.902 align:start position:0%
It felt a little hard to sleep
with all of this stuff on.

00:02:49.935 --> 00:02:52.438 align:start position:0%
I've been keeping track and
I can show you the spreadsheet.

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:55.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Like, a few basic metrics.
- Yes.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:58.311 align:start position:0%
And usually I'm getting--
I think average for a weekday

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.313 size:84% position:42%
is about 8.06 or something.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.615 align:start position:0%
And then average for
a weekend is about 8.35.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:07.720 align:start size:78% position:22%
Clearly, it looks like
that you are a reasonably
very healthy person

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:09.755 align:start size:75% position:25%
and you do take care
of your sleep very well.

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:13.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Mm-hmm.
- But it turns out that you
  have mild sleep apnea.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:18.831
So five to 15 times an hour
when someone stops breathing,

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:22.368
we say it's mild sleep apnea.
So if we can say it's 4.7--

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:26.272 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So I'm stopping
  breathing 4.7--
- 4.7 times an hour.

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:28.374 align:start size:78% position:22%
Your numbers are slightly
above the normal.

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.676 align:start size:81% position:19%
For that particular night,
your total number

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:34.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
that you stopped breathing
is around 41 times
during the night.

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:37.550 size:88% position:44%
It's shocking to hear that
I stopped breathing 41 times

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.852 align:start position:0%
in this relatively
short night of sleep.

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:43.823 align:start size:78% position:22%
When you stop breathing,
your sleep phase changes.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:46.359 align:start size:63% position:38%
It means this
is fragmented sleep.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
When you have fragmented sleep,
many times the patient

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.497 align:start size:75% position:25%
does feel sleepy, tired,
or less energetic.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.800 align:start position:0%
Just how big of a problem
is sleep loss?

00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:58.504 align:start size:88% position:13%
Every time that you are
disrupting your sleep cycle,

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:00.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
you are interrupting
your biological clock as well.

00:04:01.006 --> 00:04:03.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
And with there's a disruption,
the chaos starts.

00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:06.879 align:start size:75% position:25%
And that chaos leads
to mental health issues,

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.881 align:start size:53% position:47%
to cardiovascular
health issues.

00:04:08.914 --> 00:04:12.418 align:start size:63% position:38%
And it really starts
the aging process
much more faster.

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:16.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
Everybody has to be in sync
to live a healthy life.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.694
Okay. Um, this is what
my night of sleep looked like.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:24.063 line:0%
You can see me moving through
the four phases of sleep.

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:25.598
There is light sleep,

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:28.567
and then deep sleep
or slow wave sleep,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.503
and then these
five chunks of REM

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:31.771
or rapid eye movement sleep.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.506
That's the phase
during which we dream.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.042
I was not expecting
to be diagnosed

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.577
with a mild sleep disorder
going into this story,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.047
but it does make we want
to better understand

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.416
what Dr. Shukla mentioned
about this idea

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.818
of being in sync
with your biological clock.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.821
Everybody has a specific
bodily schedule

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.756
that determines
when they should go to sleep

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:52.525
and when they should wake up.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.793
It's called our chronotype.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.429
I wanted to figure out
what mine was.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:59.432
It turns out there are
online quizzes you can take

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.000
or genetic tests
that you can take,

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.869
but there's also a much simpler
way to figure it out.

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:07.440
<i> ( Skype ringing )</i>

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.075 line:0%
- Hello.
- Hello!

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:11.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
- How's it going?
- It's going well, man.
  How are you?

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:16.949
I have a request for you
and it's gonna potentially
be weird and scary.

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:19.618
I want you to try
for one week

00:05:19.651 --> 00:05:22.088
to turn off
your alarm entirely

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.557
and go to bed
when you're tired

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.093
and then wake up
when you are rested,

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.395
- but no alarm clock.
- That sounds amazing.

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:32.498 align:start position:0%
This is the best task
I've ever been given.

00:05:32.531 --> 00:05:34.500
That's my main assignment
for you for this week.

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:36.369 align:start size:91% position:9%
- All right, man.
  I'll see you in a week.
- Bye.

00:05:49.848 --> 00:05:52.952 line:0%
So it's about 8 AM
and I just woke up.

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:56.489 line:0%
I like the safety net
of having an alarm clock.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.023 line:0%
I slept with anxiety
all night long.

00:05:58.056 --> 00:06:02.795 line:0%
I woke up at 7:30,

00:06:02.828 --> 00:06:04.930 line:0%
way earlier than I usually do.

00:06:04.963 --> 00:06:06.999 line:0%
My bedtime is kind of going
later and later

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:08.968 line:0%
as this experiment goes on.

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.836 line:0%
Can you have a chronotype
where you actually

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:13.406 line:0%
just go to sleep
at 2 AM every day?

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.775 line:0%
I keep waking up

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.878 line:0%
way earlier than I planned on
getting out of bed.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:20.846 line:0%
So I do feel like
I'm sleeping more.

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:22.448 line:0%
I feel like
I'm sleeping better.

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:24.517 line:0%
It's really nice
not to have an alarm.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.820 line:0%
I'm really enjoying not having
an alarm to wake me up.

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:30.022 line:0%
I don't know what I'm supposed
to be learning about

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:32.158 line:0%
throughout this experience

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:37.430 line:0%
or what it's supposed to do,
but I'm exhausted.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:42.201 line:0%
Well, today was the last day
of the no alarm experiment.

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:46.138 line:0%
Spoiler alert,
I am not a morning person.

00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:47.706 line:0%
I feel a little embarrassed

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:50.709 line:0%
because I've missed
our 10 AM meeting

00:06:50.742 --> 00:06:53.446 line:0%
pretty much every day
of this experiment.

00:06:56.181 --> 00:06:58.484 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Cool.
- What is this?

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:03.088 align:start size:75% position:25%
So this is a big model
of the circadian rhythm.

00:07:03.121 --> 00:07:05.858 align:start size:81% position:19%
This is the process
that our bodies go through

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:08.227 align:start size:47% position:53%
every 24 hours
like clockwork.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.830 align:start size:81% position:19%
So what I would love to do
is just plot out

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:13.199 align:start size:59% position:41%
when we went to bed
and when we woke up

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:14.867 align:start size:56% position:44%
on average
for the past week.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.469 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Should we do it?
- Let's do it.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:19.505 align:start size:75% position:25%
I went to bed on average

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.607 align:start size:47% position:53%
at, like, 12:17

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.209 align:start size:81% position:19%
and woke up at about 8:45.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.844
Lee:
That's very precise.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.779
Mine was probably
around midnight,

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.581
and then I was getting up
every day around 8:00.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.516
Joss: And I'm the night owl
of the bunch, I guess.

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:33.853 align:start position:0%
My average bedtime
was about 1:26,

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:39.558 align:start size:75% position:25%
and my average wake time
was 10:20.

00:07:39.591 --> 00:07:41.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm so lazy.
- Let's go through it.

00:07:41.827 --> 00:07:43.095
Right about when you wake up,

00:07:43.128 --> 00:07:44.897 align:start size:63% position:38%
your body starts
to release cortisol.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:46.932
You have the sharpest rise
in blood pressure of the day,

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:49.502 align:start size:75% position:25%
and you stop producing
a the hormone melatonin.

00:07:49.535 --> 00:07:51.203
And these things all together,

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:55.574 align:start size:81% position:19%
those wake us up
and start us into the day.

00:07:55.607 --> 00:07:57.042
- Daytime.
- Daytime.

00:07:57.075 --> 00:07:59.211
So then intestinal movement
begins for the day.

00:07:59.244 --> 00:08:02.748
The morning poop-- biologically
scheduled into your day.

00:08:02.781 --> 00:08:05.050 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right after
  that 8 AM coffee.
- Right, exactly.

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:08.153
After that, your body is at
its peak alertness of the day.

00:08:08.186 --> 00:08:11.156
Then there's this blank gap.
It's the afternoon slump.

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:14.627
A few hours later,
your body has its best
coordination of the day,

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.829
followed by its quickest
reaction time.

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:20.099
Olympic athletes, for example,
have their highest odds

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:22.001
of beating a record

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:24.136
if they are
performing the sport

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.871
exactly within this window.

00:08:25.904 --> 00:08:29.041
We near sunset,
and then your blood pressure

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:30.643
reaches its daily peak

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.645
followed by peak
body temperature.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:36.649 align:start position:0%
So all of our bodies
are doing this
on this 24-hour cycle,

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.052 align:start position:0%
But in terms of how that relates
to our time and our clock,

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:42.087 align:start position:0%
it can vary from person
to person?

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah. Your body is probably
most alert at 1 PM,

00:08:45.657 --> 00:08:48.994 align:start size:75% position:25%
whereas mine is probably
most alert at noon

00:08:49.027 --> 00:08:52.298 align:start size:81% position:19%
and Lee's is probably most
alert closer to 11 AM.

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:56.235
If you are a night owl,
this entire cascade of events

00:08:56.268 --> 00:08:59.238
shifts forward in time.

00:08:59.271 --> 00:09:02.308
And if you're an early bird,
it shifts back in time.

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:04.043 align:start size:63% position:38%
And then it's night.

00:09:04.076 --> 00:09:05.778 align:start size:63% position:38%
Your body starts
to produce melatonin

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.212
getting you ready for bed.

00:09:07.245 --> 00:09:08.647
A few hours into sleeping,

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:10.182
you fall into
your deepest sleep,

00:09:10.215 --> 00:09:13.052
and then you fall into
your lowest body temperature.

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:14.587
And that's it.
That's the whole cycle.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.656 align:start size:66% position:34%
And when we don't
follow that schedule,

00:09:17.689 --> 00:09:19.325
especially when
we cut sleep short,

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:23.228
we're derailing every other
thing in the cycle.

00:09:23.261 --> 00:09:29.234
So, this chart shows
the distribution of chronotypes.

00:09:29.267 --> 00:09:31.337
And the way that
they measure that

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:34.239
is by looking at
the mid-sleep time
on free days.

00:09:34.272 --> 00:09:36.308 align:start size:56% position:44%
So you'll see that
for most people,

00:09:36.341 --> 00:09:38.644
their midpoint is about 4 AM,

00:09:38.677 --> 00:09:40.312
which means that
they're sleeping from, like--

00:09:40.345 --> 00:09:42.047 align:start size:91% position:9%
- This is assuming
  they sleep eight hours?
- Yeah.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.883 align:start size:63% position:38%
But for the majority
of people,

00:09:44.916 --> 00:09:47.353 align:start size:53% position:47%
getting to, like,
an 8 or 9 AM job,

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:50.856
means waking up before
their body is ready.

00:09:50.889 --> 00:09:53.892 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Wow.
- Where that gets
  really concerning

00:09:53.925 --> 00:09:59.698
is with the health impacts
of this distribution.

00:09:59.731 --> 00:10:01.266 align:start size:69% position:31%
The later
that midsleep time is,

00:10:01.299 --> 00:10:03.902
the higher the rates
of depression,

00:10:03.935 --> 00:10:05.371
of people who smoke,

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:07.973
and of people
who consume alcohol.

00:10:08.006 --> 00:10:10.242 align:start position:0%
If it's the majority of us
who are suffering,

00:10:10.275 --> 00:10:11.844 align:start position:0%
why are we doing this?

00:10:11.877 --> 00:10:15.147 align:start size:72% position:28%
When we have a world
that is so preferential

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.082 align:end position:100%
towards early birds
and geared towards

00:10:17.115 --> 00:10:19.284 align:start size:47% position:53%
productivity
in the morning,

00:10:19.317 --> 00:10:23.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
the people who don't fit in
to that really suffer.

00:10:23.088 --> 00:10:24.090
Yeah.

00:10:27.092 --> 00:10:30.829
Christophe:
So you study the sleep-wake
cycles that control

00:10:30.862 --> 00:10:32.965 align:start size:56% position:44%
every living thing
in the world.

00:10:32.998 --> 00:10:35.401 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
What do you think are
some of the big things

00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:38.103 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
that you wish people
understood better

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:41.106 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
about their ability to get
a good night of sleep?

00:10:41.139 --> 00:10:44.977
Our genome and almost every gene
in our genome is programmed

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:48.747
to turn on and turn off
at a certain time
of the day or night.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:52.117
So that means
we are programmed to sleep
a certain number of hours.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.888
When we follow these rhythms,
our body can perform

00:10:55.921 --> 00:10:59.391
at its peak performance
during the daytime

00:10:59.424 --> 00:11:03.929
and can reset, repair,
and regenerate at nighttime
when we go to sleep.

00:11:03.962 --> 00:11:06.165
So that means
when we don't sleep,

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:10.703
it actually has huge cascading
bad effects on our kidney,

00:11:10.736 --> 00:11:13.305
liver, gut, intestine, heart,

00:11:13.338 --> 00:11:15.974
even our skin, how we look.

00:11:16.007 --> 00:11:17.476 align:start position:0%
Right. Beauty sleep.

00:11:17.509 --> 00:11:23.148
We can figure out how to
be the master of our own clock

00:11:23.181 --> 00:11:27.352
by adopting very simple
lifestyle changes

00:11:27.385 --> 00:11:31.156
such as when to wake up,
when to turn on and turn off
your bright light,

00:11:31.189 --> 00:11:33.292
when to eat,
and when to stop eating.

00:11:33.325 --> 00:11:37.329
In a very simple sense,
if you have the same schedule
every single day,

00:11:37.362 --> 00:11:39.798
or at least five
to six days in a week,

00:11:39.831 --> 00:11:41.867
that itself
is a very powerful way

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:43.802
to be in control
of your circadian rhythm.

00:11:43.835 --> 00:11:45.904 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean,
all this is so fascinating
because a lot of us

00:11:45.937 --> 00:11:48.340 align:start size:72% position:28%
think that we can sleep
whenever we want,

00:11:48.373 --> 00:11:52.177 size:88% position:56%
but it sounds like the body
is a whole lot less flexible

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.879 align:start size:81% position:19%
than we think it might be.

00:11:53.912 --> 00:11:56.982
This whole idea that we can
have 24/7 food delivery,

00:11:57.015 --> 00:12:00.853
24/7 drug stores
and grocery stores,

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:03.388
those are all based on
the simple idea

00:12:03.421 --> 00:12:07.159
that we can take our body
for a spin at any given time
of the day or night.

00:12:07.192 --> 00:12:09.495
And it's becoming
more and more clear

00:12:09.528 --> 00:12:13.866
that if you don't have control
over when you work,

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:17.169
then you also don't have control
over how much you sleep.

00:12:17.202 --> 00:12:20.205
It has come to a point that
the World Health Organization

00:12:20.238 --> 00:12:23.542
has now designated shift work
as a carcinogen.

00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:27.546 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Wow.
- Just imagine, just doing
  shift work is the same

00:12:27.579 --> 00:12:31.483
as getting exposed
to cancer-causing chemicals
in the environment.

00:12:31.516 --> 00:12:37.423 align:start position:0%
And that's very profound.
That says how important sleep
and circadian rhythms are.

00:12:40.492 --> 00:12:43.228
So, shift work can be so bad
for the human body

00:12:43.261 --> 00:12:46.231
that it's been classified
as a carcinogen.

00:12:46.264 --> 00:12:49.168
But it's not a problem
that everybody has to
deal with equally.

00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:51.537
In the U.S., white people
are overrepresented

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.540
among those who start work
at around 7 and 8 AM.

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:57.342
Black and LatinX people are
overrepresented among workers

00:12:57.375 --> 00:12:59.077
who start
in the early morning.

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:01.246
And black people are
especially overrepresented

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:03.482
among those who start work
late at night.

00:13:03.515 --> 00:13:06.385
I'm on my way right now to talk
to Dr. Giradin Jean-Louis.

00:13:06.418 --> 00:13:09.922
He's a professor at NYU who
studies the health consequences

00:13:09.955 --> 00:13:11.023
of this racial sleep gap.

00:13:13.525 --> 00:13:15.427 align:start size:47% position:53%
Can you sort of
paint a picture

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:17.429 align:start size:56% position:44%
of what sleep
health disparities

00:13:17.462 --> 00:13:19.364 align:start size:69% position:31%
look like in the U.S.?

00:13:19.397 --> 00:13:22.568 align:start size:91% position:9%
Who gets to sleep well in
this country and who doesn't?

00:13:22.601 --> 00:13:25.270 size:75% position:38% line:0%
What we have been seeing
over the last 50 years

00:13:25.303 --> 00:13:28.273 size:84% position:42% line:0%
is that there's a disparity
between blacks and white

00:13:28.306 --> 00:13:29.608 align:start position:0% line:0%
in terms of how much
sleep they get.

00:13:29.641 --> 00:13:32.044 align:start position:0%
And we're finding
that blacks overall

00:13:32.077 --> 00:13:34.046 align:start position:0%
seem to be sleeping
about 30 minutes less.

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:36.415 align:start position:0%
And of course that
would have repercussions.

00:13:36.448 --> 00:13:38.050 align:start position:0%
You're gonna be at
greater risk of obesity,

00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:40.452 align:start position:0%
for diabetes, hypertension,
and now we've seen

00:13:40.485 --> 00:13:42.354 align:start position:0%
the same is true
for Alzheimer's, dementia.

00:13:42.387 --> 00:13:44.456 align:start size:59% position:41%
Can you walk me
through the factors

00:13:44.489 --> 00:13:48.193
that are particularly hitting
black Americans the hardest?

00:13:48.226 --> 00:13:50.128 size:88% position:44%
Well, socioeconomic factors,

00:13:50.161 --> 00:13:52.965 align:start position:0%
environmental factors,
psychosocial factors.

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:56.301 size:75% position:38%
So, for instance, if you
find that in a community

00:13:56.334 --> 00:13:59.471 align:start position:0%
perceived racism is an issue,
well, it's going to
affect your sleep.

00:13:59.504 --> 00:14:01.974 align:start position:0%
If you happen to be in
an environment where there's
quite a bit of noise,

00:14:02.007 --> 00:14:03.208
it's gonna affect your sleep.

00:14:03.241 --> 00:14:05.978 align:start position:0%
If you have light pollution,
air pollution,

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:08.146 align:start position:0%
all of those factors
can influence your sleep.

00:14:08.179 --> 00:14:12.017 align:start position:0%
And we also find that
there are a lot of black folks
that are working two jobs.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.620 align:start position:0%
So the ability to actually
set a schedule--

00:14:14.653 --> 00:14:17.022 align:start position:0%
you're gonna go to bed
at the same time,
wake up at the same time,

00:14:17.055 --> 00:14:19.625
which is critically important--
they don't have that luxury.

00:14:22.227 --> 00:14:25.931 align:start size:72% position:28%
So this is a map
of short sleep duration
by census tract,

00:14:25.964 --> 00:14:30.035
so this is where people are
getting less than seven hours
of sleep per night regularly.

00:14:30.068 --> 00:14:33.205 align:start size:81% position:19%
Can you help me understand
why this map looks
the way it does?

00:14:33.238 --> 00:14:35.507 size:88% position:44%
All the areas that are dark
suggest that these are folks

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:37.509 align:start position:0%
who are having significant
problems sleeping.

00:14:37.542 --> 00:14:40.379 align:start position:0%
And as we get lighter
and lighter, these are people
that are doing okay.

00:14:40.412 --> 00:14:43.615 align:start size:75% position:25%
So what you want to see
is communities like this
seem to be doing well,

00:14:43.648 --> 00:14:46.985 align:start size:69% position:31%
whereas communities
like this or like that
are not doing so well.

00:14:47.018 --> 00:14:51.056 align:start position:0%
And why is that important?
Because the higher the
prevalence of short sleepers,

00:14:51.089 --> 00:14:53.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
the more likely you're
going to be seeing obesity,
high blood pressure,

00:14:54.025 --> 00:14:57.029 align:start size:91% position:9%
diabetes, and to some degree,
cardiovascular disease,

00:14:57.062 --> 00:14:59.631
stroke,
and myocardial infarction.

00:14:59.664 --> 00:15:03.302
What we also note
is if you're looking at
the obesity belt,

00:15:03.335 --> 00:15:06.505
this section also is where
most people happen to be obese.

00:15:06.538 --> 00:15:09.341 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Hmm.
- So there seems to be
  direct correlation

00:15:09.374 --> 00:15:11.443 align:start position:0%
between obesity
and poor sleep.

00:15:11.476 --> 00:15:15.547
So it seems like the places
that really stand out to me

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.716
are in the southeast,

00:15:17.749 --> 00:15:20.118
all along the Mississippi
in the south here,

00:15:20.151 --> 00:15:23.055
this Kentucky-
West Virginia cluster.

00:15:23.088 --> 00:15:24.423 align:start position:0%
Those are really
the deepest reds,

00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:26.224 align:start size:50% position:50%
not to mention
also right here,

00:15:26.257 --> 00:15:28.026 align:start size:78% position:22%
which are Native American
reservations.

00:15:28.059 --> 00:15:29.261 align:start position:0%
So here's what's
interesting now.

00:15:29.294 --> 00:15:31.430 align:start size:56% position:44%
When we're looking
at COVID data,

00:15:31.463 --> 00:15:34.967 align:start position:0%
we find that a lot
of those people that are
infected with COVID

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.503
happen to be in the same area
with people having short sleep.

00:15:38.536 --> 00:15:41.340 align:start position:0%
So we know what's
happening here is that
if you're not sleeping enough,

00:15:41.373 --> 00:15:43.075 align:start position:0%
your immune system
is compromised,

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:45.243
so you're more likely
to get a COVID-19 infection

00:15:45.276 --> 00:15:46.578
and die as a result of it.

00:15:46.611 --> 00:15:50.382 align:start size:63% position:38%
It sounds like sleep
is a basic need

00:15:50.415 --> 00:15:54.186 align:start size:66% position:34%
that in a lot of ways
has been turned
into a privilege.

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:56.588 align:start position:0%
If your circadian rhythms
are not robust,

00:15:56.621 --> 00:15:59.257 align:start position:0%
the ability to fight off
certain diseases,

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:02.361 align:start position:0%
your ability to remain
metabolically healthy

00:16:02.394 --> 00:16:04.596 size:88% position:44%
is significantly compromised
because the circadian clock

00:16:04.629 --> 00:16:06.598 size:88% position:44%
controls biological rhythms.

00:16:06.631 --> 00:16:09.267 align:start position:0%
So when you bring
those two factors together,

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.603 align:start position:0%
it makes it absolutely
difficult for a black person

00:16:11.636 --> 00:16:14.540 size:78% position:39%
to actually stay healthy.

00:16:17.375 --> 00:16:19.378
( vibrating )

00:16:21.479 --> 00:16:23.482
You know, at least
for the past few decades,

00:16:23.515 --> 00:16:26.618 align:start size:78% position:22%
there's never been, like,
a shortage of things
to wake you up,

00:16:26.651 --> 00:16:28.186 align:start size:69% position:31%
things to
put you back to sleep.

00:16:28.219 --> 00:16:30.288 line:0%
The truth is
that for most of my life,

00:16:30.321 --> 00:16:32.424 line:0%
I haven't had
that much control over
when I needed to wake up.

00:16:32.457 --> 00:16:34.626
If we want
to address sleep loss

00:16:34.659 --> 00:16:36.728
as the public
health problem that it is,

00:16:36.761 --> 00:16:39.698 align:start size:75% position:25%
we have to do it through
structural changes
and policy changes,

00:16:39.731 --> 00:16:41.767 align:start size:59% position:41%
not just through
individual changes.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.136 align:start size:72% position:28%
There are some policies
that we know can make
a difference.

00:16:46.638 --> 00:16:49.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the first example has to do
with how we schedule work.

00:16:49.507 --> 00:16:52.644 align:start size:81% position:19%
In 2015,
there was this fascinating
real world experiment

00:16:52.677 --> 00:16:55.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
conducted on
a steel factory in Germany.

00:16:55.713 --> 00:16:59.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
Basically, all the workers
normally would cycle through
three different shifts.

00:16:59.617 --> 00:17:03.722
There was a morning shift
that was from 6 AM to 2 PM,

00:17:03.755 --> 00:17:07.325 align:start size:72% position:28%
an evening shift
that was 2 PM to 10 PM,

00:17:07.358 --> 00:17:11.296
and then a night shift
that was 10 PM to 6 AM.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:13.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Got it.
- So they would cycle
  two days of each

00:17:13.331 --> 00:17:15.500
and then a couple
days off in between.

00:17:15.533 --> 00:17:19.171
Two days of each?
Like, they could never
establish a routine?

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:21.173 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah, yeah.
- Oof.

00:17:21.206 --> 00:17:23.141 line:0%
On this chart,
each one of these

00:17:23.174 --> 00:17:24.509 line:0%
is basically a morning person,

00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:27.145 line:0%
someone whose chronotype
is very skewed early.

00:17:27.178 --> 00:17:30.315 line:0%
Each one of these is someone
who has an average chronotype.

00:17:30.348 --> 00:17:32.684 line:0%
And then each of these
is a late chronotype.

00:17:32.717 --> 00:17:35.520 line:0%
So, a night owl.
Very late sleeper.

00:17:35.553 --> 00:17:37.589 line:0%
Then researchers came in,
and what they did

00:17:37.622 --> 00:17:41.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
is they basically reassigned
shifts by chronotype.

00:17:41.726 --> 00:17:44.663
So every single late sleeper

00:17:44.696 --> 00:17:47.799
was made to never work
the morning shift.

00:17:47.832 --> 00:17:49.434
And then every early bird

00:17:49.467 --> 00:17:51.603 line:0%
was made to never have
to work the night shift.

00:17:51.636 --> 00:17:53.605
And the results were really,
really fascinating.

00:17:53.638 --> 00:17:56.141
So for both
the early chronotypes
and the late chronotypes,

00:17:56.174 --> 00:17:58.677 line:0%
sleep duration
on workdays went up,

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.212 line:0%
sleep quality went up,

00:18:00.245 --> 00:18:02.514 line:0%
and their well-being
on workdays went up.

00:18:02.547 --> 00:18:06.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
- This looks really positive.
- There's a really strong
  case that employers

00:18:06.918 --> 00:18:08.720
should help their employees

00:18:08.753 --> 00:18:11.223 align:start size:66% position:34%
sleep according
to their chronotypes.

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:13.892 align:end position:100%
It makes a huge difference
on their physical health,

00:18:13.925 --> 00:18:16.461 align:start size:63% position:38%
their mental health,
their productivity,
their happiness.

00:18:16.494 --> 00:18:18.296 align:start size:91% position:9%
And you do see different
versions of this catching on.

00:18:18.329 --> 00:18:21.266 align:start size:91% position:9%
Southwest Airlines
apparently lets pilots choose

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:23.502 align:start size:69% position:31%
between an AM schedule
versus a PM schedule.

00:18:23.535 --> 00:18:25.403 align:start size:75% position:25%
For a lot of these jobs,

00:18:25.436 --> 00:18:27.372 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's about safety of
everyone else around them.

00:18:27.405 --> 00:18:29.741 align:start position:0%
When I look at this,
I think the time in my life

00:18:29.774 --> 00:18:32.844 align:start position:0%
when I was most
schedule constrained
was when I was in school.

00:18:32.877 --> 00:18:34.880 align:start position:0%
Are schools doing
anything different now?

00:18:34.913 --> 00:18:38.350 line:0%
Yeah. These same chronotype
optimizing benefits

00:18:38.383 --> 00:18:41.453
are very apparent
in school start time.

00:18:41.486 --> 00:18:46.291
So this is roughly what
the adolescent chronotype
looks like.

00:18:46.324 --> 00:18:50.228 align:start size:81% position:19%
The average teen
needs between eight to ten
hours of sleep.

00:18:50.261 --> 00:18:52.898
And then this is what
the average school schedule
looks like.

00:18:52.931 --> 00:18:54.599
The American Academy of
Pediatrics suggests

00:18:54.632 --> 00:18:56.835
that school should never
start before 8:30.

00:18:56.868 --> 00:18:59.271 line:0%
So it's really this overlap

00:18:59.304 --> 00:19:02.307 line:0%
that is just wreaking havoc
on the teenage body.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.509 align:start position:0%
They're doing calculus
during their sleep schedule.

00:19:04.542 --> 00:19:08.914 align:start size:81% position:19%
Mm-hmm, entirely.
So we're requiring kids to
basically come into school

00:19:08.947 --> 00:19:11.316 align:start size:72% position:28%
way before their bodies
are ready for it.

00:19:11.349 --> 00:19:13.385 align:start size:69% position:31%
There have been dozens
of studies looking

00:19:13.418 --> 00:19:15.587 align:start size:63% position:38%
at what happens when
you try to fix this.

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:19.191
Delaying start times is
associated with an increase
in sleep duration,

00:19:19.224 --> 00:19:21.626
improved attendance,
better grades,

00:19:21.659 --> 00:19:24.629
better graduation rates,
lower rates of depression,

00:19:24.662 --> 00:19:26.831
and even fewer
motor vehicle crashes.

00:19:26.864 --> 00:19:29.467 align:start size:66% position:34%
It's a long list
of really good things

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.803 align:start size:75% position:25%
from just one extra hour
of sleep in the morning.

00:19:31.836 --> 00:19:34.906 align:start position:0%
You just listed everything
that's important for teenagers.

00:19:34.939 --> 00:19:37.309 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- It feels like parents
  should care a lot

00:19:37.342 --> 00:19:39.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
- about making this change.
- Yeah.

00:19:39.911 --> 00:19:41.813 align:start position:0%
Also, I gotta say,
it doesn't seem like

00:19:41.846 --> 00:19:43.748 align:start position:0%
the hardest change
in the world to make.

00:19:43.781 --> 00:19:45.483
And these are just
a start, right?

00:19:45.516 --> 00:19:48.286
This is just the beginning
of what a world

00:19:48.319 --> 00:19:51.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
that takes chronotype into
account when it comes to work,

00:19:51.823 --> 00:19:53.625 align:start size:81% position:19%
when it comes
to scheduling, everything,

00:19:53.658 --> 00:19:55.227 size:88% position:56%
what that world
could look like.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.629 align:start size:69% position:31%
It doesn't necessarily
sound easy,

00:19:57.662 --> 00:20:00.832 align:start size:63% position:38%
but it does sound
incredibly worth it.

00:20:03.301 --> 00:20:05.670
I started this story
hoping to figure out

00:20:05.703 --> 00:20:09.507
why so many people struggle
to get a good night's sleep.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.777
And the answer
that I'm left with
is kind of frustrating.

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.379
You know, getting
the right amount of sleep

00:20:14.412 --> 00:20:16.748
at the right time
for your circadian clock,

00:20:16.781 --> 00:20:19.317
it's one of the most important
things that you can do
for your body.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:23.355 align:start position:0%
Sleep is foundational.
Anything in life requires
a good night's sleep.

00:20:23.388 --> 00:20:24.856 align:start position:0%
So if you're
not sleeping enough,

00:20:24.889 --> 00:20:26.925
everything is really on hold.

00:20:26.958 --> 00:20:30.862
But the thing is,
our schedules and our habits

00:20:30.895 --> 00:20:32.797
and our culture
and our environments,

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:36.534
all those things get
in the way of us doing so.

00:20:36.567 --> 00:20:38.703
But when we trivialize sleep,

00:20:38.736 --> 00:20:41.273
we're basically pretending
that our biological limits

00:20:41.306 --> 00:20:43.475
matter a lot less
than they actually do.

00:20:43.508 --> 00:20:47.512
Public policy, workplace policy,
school policy,

00:20:47.545 --> 00:20:53.418 align:start position:0%
all these policies
have to change so that
we can have a more coherent

00:20:53.451 --> 00:20:56.721
and synergistic effort going on
throughout the society

00:20:56.754 --> 00:20:59.357
to nurture
our circadian rhythms.

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:01.026
Does that mean that my takeaway
is that we all need

00:21:01.059 --> 00:21:04.396
to turn off our alarm clocks
and sleep whenever we want?

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:08.066
No, obviously not.
But there are thing that
we can do to help the people

00:21:08.099 --> 00:21:09.901
suffering the most
from sleep loss to have

00:21:09.934 --> 00:21:14.406
the time and the flexibility
to get rest when they need it.

00:21:14.439 --> 00:21:16.941
I think that's
a good place to start.

00:21:16.974 --> 00:21:22.047 align:start position:0% line:0%
I feel like I am not
gonna have the greatest
night of sleep.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.883 align:start position:0% line:0%
Oh, I don't like that.

00:21:24.916 --> 00:21:26.584 align:start position:0% line:0%
So about two hours
after you wake up,

00:21:26.617 --> 00:21:28.053 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- that's when you
  should go to the gym?
- Mm-hmm.

00:21:28.086 --> 00:21:29.888 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
That's interesting
information.

